  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/acestrorhynchus_cf_minimus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The barracuda tetras (Acestrorhynchus) from South America are beautiful predators, which are much sought for among specialized aquarists with large tanks. Several of the approximately 14 species accepted currently become 20 to 40 cm long, but there do exist also small species in the genus that become never larger than 10 cm.</p><p>Origin: Peru. Available size: 6-8 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/acestrorhynchus_cf_minimus_en/",
      "html": "<p>A. minimus becomes only 6-8 cm long and so the species fits very well in a 200 litre tank. On the other hand the tank should not be much smaller, as barracuda tetras are active hunters and thus need some space.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/acestrorhynchus_cf_minimus_en/",
      "html": "<p>They should be fed with small feeder fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/acestrorhynchus_cf_minimus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Acestrorhynchus are comparably peaceful predators and should be kept in schools.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/acestrorhynchus_cf_minimus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Acestrorhynchus: from ancient Greek, means “needle snout”. <em>minimus</em>: Latin, means “the smallest”.</p><p>Suggestion of a common name: Dwarf barracuda tetra</p>"
    }
  ],
